@startuml
!include ../../styles.puml

title State Machine Pattern

header
The State Machine design pattern (also known as the State pattern) is a behavioral design pattern
that allows an object to change its behavior dynamically based on its internal state.
Instead of using conditional statements (like if-else or switch) to handle different states,
the pattern encapsulates state-specific behavior into separate classes. This makes the code
more modular, easier to maintain, and extensible (e.g., adding new states without modifying existing code).
endheader

class TrafficLight {
    - _state: TrafficLightState
    + __init__()
    + set_state(state: TrafficLightState)
    + change_state()
}

abstract class TrafficLightState {
    + {abstract} handle(context: TrafficLight)
}

class RedLightState extends TrafficLightState {
    + handle(context: TrafficLight)
}

class GreenLightState extends TrafficLightState {
    + handle(context: TrafficLight)
}

class YellowLightState extends TrafficLightState {
    + handle(context: TrafficLight)
}

TrafficLight --> TrafficLightState : _state

@enduml
